  <Fragment slot="lede">Look at how the call was made before you look at how it turned out. Good calls sometimes start badly, and bad ones sometimes get lucky.</Fragment>

  <h2>The short answer</h2>

  <p>Grade the decision first and the result second. Good product decisions tend to show four signs. Clear principles guide them. Little stands in their way. Input comes in on a few agreed risks, not every opinion in the building. And the team can explain why the call was right, even before the results catch up. Most of the time, you only need <a href="https://www.aboutamazon.com/news/company-news/2016-letter-to-shareholders" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">about 70% of the information</a> you'd like.</p>

  <h2>Why decision quality matters</h2>

  <p>Setting up the conditions for good decisions is one of the main things a product leader is for. Every product person makes decisions all day, every day. Few get taught how.</p>

  <p>It shows up in the numbers, too. <a href="https://hbr.org/2010/06/the-decision-driven-organization" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">One study of companies</a> with revenue above $1 billion found decision effectiveness and financial results moved together at a 95% confidence level.</p>

  <p>Plenty of companies measure engineering speed down to the story point. Very few measure how fast they decide. Slow decisions drag a culture down as surely as slow code.</p>

  <p>AI raises the stakes. Teams count how often an AI feature gets used and how many minutes a task saves. They rarely check whether the decisions got any better. <a href="/writing/the-case-for-measuring-decision-quality/">That's the measure worth adding</a>.</p>

  <h2>Sign 1: principles shape decisions</h2>

  <p>Lots of teams agree on the problem and still can't agree on a decision. What's missing is a shared way to weigh the options.</p>

  <p>When I coach new leaders, this is usually the weakest muscle. They polish their own idea, or argue their corner, when the better move is to lay the tradeoffs side by side.</p>

  <p>Most business choices aren't good against bad. They're good against good. <a href="https://medium.com/the-ready/even-overs-the-prioritization-tool-that-brings-your-strategy-to-life-e4f28f2949ac" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Even-over statements</a> make that explicit by saying which good thing wins:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Retention even over acquisition</li>
    <li>Depth in one market even over reach across many</li>
    <li>Learning fast even over launching polished</li>
  </ul>

  <p>A good principle can flip when the strategy changes. A company protecting its base puts retention first. The same company, chasing a new market, might put acquisition first. When a discussion stalls, or someone won't budge, go back to the principles.</p>

  <h2>Sign 2: barriers are few</h2>

  <p>Small decisions should be quick. These are the usual reasons they aren't:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Waiting for "all the data"</li>
    <li>Whoever has the most influence wins, not the customer or the business</li>
    <li>Big, lasting decisions get made without the people they affect</li>
    <li>So many voices that the committee decides, and nobody owns it</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Ask your team where they get stuck before the next step. Then go and look.</p>

  <h2>Sign 3: input is structured</h2>

  <p>At the start of most projects, everyone knows what matters. A few months in, the team is fielding a dozen opinions, dozens of risks and an approval chain measured in months.</p>

  <p>The fix is what I call structured input. Stakeholders weigh in on the handful of risks that matter most, and the team gets on with the rest. <a href="https://www.svpg.com/four-big-risks/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Marty Cagan's four risks</a> make a good starting set: value, usability, feasibility and business viability.</p>

  <p>Most decisions don't need a meeting. Set the big decision points early. Be clear about who advises, who shapes and who decides. Once it's decided, everyone backs it. Keep the process light.</p>

  <h2>Sign 4: you can judge the call, not just the result</h2>

  <p>What good looks like will differ from one organisation to the next. It comes down to the inputs, the outputs and the way decisions move between people and, now, machines. LLMs help here in an odd way: they push attention back onto the work itself and away from the work about the work.</p>

  <p>Five questions to test a decision:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>What's the context?</li>
    <li>How does everyone know what matters most?</li>
    <li>What will your teams do with that?</li>
    <li>What does success look like, and how will you measure it?</li>
    <li>If the early results disappoint, how would you know it was still the right call?</li>
  </ol>

  <p>A team that can answer the last one is judging its decisions, not just its luck.</p>

  <h2>When to bring someone in</h2>

  <p>If the same barriers keep stalling decisions, or nobody senior owns how product calls get made, that's a product leadership gap. A <a href="/fractional/">fractional product leader</a> can put the principles, decision points and measures in place, then hand them to the team.</p>

